Vulnerability Title

Improper Server-Side Validation of Confirmation Timeout Interval Parameter

Vulnerability Category
Business Logic Vulnerability
Input Validation Failure
Parameter Tampering
OWASP Category (OWASP Top 10 - 2021/2025 Mapping)
A01:2021 – Broken Access Control (if restriction bypass impacts workflow/security controls)
A04:2021 – Insecure Design
A05:2021 – Security Misconfiguration (secondary mapping)
CVSS v3.1 Score
CVSS:3.1/AV:N/AC:L/PR:L/UI:N/S:U/C:L/I:H/A:L
Base Score: 7.1 (High)
CVSS Vector Breakdown
AV:N – Exploitable over network
AC:L – Low attack complexity
PR:L – Requires authenticated user access
UI:N – No victim interaction required
S:U – Scope unchanged
C:L – Limited confidentiality impact
I:H – High integrity impact due to manipulation of workflow rules
A:L – Minor availability/process impact
Description

The application provides predefined selectable interval values for the Confirmation Agreed Timeout Interval field through the UI dropdown, such as 15, 30, 45, 60, 90, and 120 minutes.

However, during testing it was identified that the application does not enforce proper server-side validation for this parameter. By intercepting the request using Burp Suite and modifying the interval value from an allowed value (e.g., 15) to an unauthorized value such as 500, the application successfully accepts and processes the modified request.

This indicates that validation is enforced only at the client-side/UI level and can be bypassed through parameter tampering.

Steps to Reproduce
Login to the application with valid credentials.
Navigate to the confirmation configuration section.
Observe that the interval dropdown only allows predefined values:
15 Minutes
30 Minutes
45 Minutes
60 Minutes
90 Minutes
120 Minutes
Intercept the request using Burp Suite.

Modify the interval parameter value from:

interval=15

to:

interval=500
Forward the request to the server.
Observe that the application accepts and stores/processes the unauthorized interval value.
Impact

Successful exploitation may allow attackers or unauthorized users to manipulate business workflow timing controls beyond intended limits. This could result in:

Bypass of business rules and operational restrictions
Delayed confirmations or timeout enforcement
Abuse of application workflows
Potential denial of operational processes
Inconsistent system behavior and audit inaccuracies
Risk of downstream processing failures if dependent systems expect only predefined values

If timeout intervals are security-sensitive, attackers may intentionally prolong transaction validity windows or bypass expected expiration controls.

Mitigation

Implement strict server-side validation for the interval parameter.

Recommended controls:

Validate incoming interval values against an allowlist:

[15, 30, 45, 60, 90, 120]
Reject any value outside approved limits with proper error handling.
Do not rely solely on client-side dropdown restrictions.
Implement centralized input validation for all business-critical parameters.
Log and monitor parameter tampering attempts.
Apply schema validation/API contract enforcement at backend services.

Example:

if (!allowedIntervals.contains(interval)) {
    throw new ValidationException("Invalid interval value");
}
